how's this laptop only twenty seven  hundred dollars with this amount of  hardware built in  what is the catch well today i will let  you guys know everything with my review  of the main gear vector pro laptop  featuring amd's best mobile processor  the 5900x and rtx 3080 with 64 gigabytes  of ram and 2 terabytes of ssd all for  2700  i'll be talking about the positives as  well as some limitations and give you  guys a few tips on how to make the most  out of this laptop now with that i'll be  comparing it to the razer blade because  that is the other laptop that we've been  testing with an rtx 3080 and showing you  guys the benefits of both now that one  is a lot more expensive it's 3 400  and it comes with half of the ssd and  half the ram along with a limited rtx  3080 so you guys will see the  performance compared with both of these  systems now before i jump in i want to  thank microcenter and amd for sending us  this laptop to make this review micro  center has 25 stores nationwide with an  impressive variety of electronics from  gaming vr computer parts like processors  graphics and everything else needed to  build a pc they also have a huge variety  of desktops laptops and all the  accessories that you need not only that  but highly trained staff and low prices  check out a local micro center today to  get your hands on a wide variety of  products before purchasing or use the  link down below to check out the vector  pro at microcenter.com they're also  giving away a free 240 gigabyte ssd to  new customers so go ahead and get all  the info using the link down in the  video description getting started with  hardware on the left hand side we have a  microphone and a headphone input along  with a usb port and then on the right  hand side we have two more usb type a's  we have a micro sd card reader and on  the back we have a beer plug for the  charger we have an hdmi port and an  ethernet port it's pretty rare to see  ethernet built in but it is really nice  to have along with hdmi  and i actually do like that it's on the  back because you can keep it out of the  way when you're using the laptop and you  will need to keep this laptop plugged in  which i'll talk about in just a bit now  as far as the setup up here the keyboard  is surprisingly good at first it just  felt a little bit weird to me but when i  got used to it i was typing fast and  accurate and the trackpad that is about  average it is a diving board design and  i ended up using it with just the  tapping method and with that it was  pretty responsive and with that the  build quality is surprisingly good as  well this is really lightweight they  designed it to be light and at 17 inches  it is only five pounds making it only  0.2 pounds heavier than a 16 inch  macbook pro and onto the screen i was  really happy to see windows hello built  in i love being able to log in quickly  without having to use my fingerprint or  typing in a password now below that we  have this 17 inch screen it is a matte  display so there's really no issues with  reflectivity as with the razor and it's  also that same 400 nits but without that  glossy cover now it's not a 4k display  it's a 1440p display which does save  some battery life i'll talk about that  in just a bit and it is also a 160  hertz so it's super smooth great for  gaming and one thing that i was really  kind of worried about was the  color accuracy of the display but it is  a 100 srgb panel so if you're doing  photo or video editing you can trust  looking at this display so overall i'm  surprised that i would actually take  this screen although i do like the 4k  resolution just because it's a much more  usable display you don't have to worry  about the color accuracy shifting when  you adjust brightness and as far as the  webcam and microphones well you guys can  hear and see it for yourselves the  webcam is not great it's not the worst  i've seen though but the microphones are  pretty bad so i would suggest using a  different microphone if you're going to  be zoom conferencing the same thing goes  for the speakers they're decently loud  but the sound quality just is not great  at least if we're comparing it to  macbooks or even the razer that thing  lacks bass but it does still sound  better of course with that it's just  showing us that this machine here is  designed to do two things one be very  powerful and two  pretty much meet a low price point  especially for that performance so with  that let's jump into performance  one thing i want to mention is that  there is no thunderbolt port on this amd  system but as far as performance i was  surprised that our 200 gigabyte transfer  actually finished faster than with the  razer the razer ended up throttling the  slc cache ran out on the ssd and it took  longer even though i had a thunderbolt  connection compared to just usb type-c  and those ssds are really fast in that  raid zero mode and they are fully  upgradable their m.2 ssds as is the ram  so if you want to upgrade in the future  you can do so now for simple tasks and  web browsing this machine flies through  it that 5900 hx is super quick  so no issues there but if you go ahead  and unplug it and use it as a laptop i  saw a dip of about 25  in order to save battery life now that  does suck to lower up the performance  but compared to that razer it was  actually much faster and had less  performance drop full cpu performance is  also very good close to our m1 max and  an i9 while using less power than the i9  and with that i want to mention the fans  this cooling system works very well but  it is also very loud especially out of  the box so we had no thermal throttling  we're in a full 60 watts the whole time  no frequency drops and the system stayed  cool as well but it was loud now i did a  bunch of testing and i found that the  stock fan curves they're just excessive  you know you're just running way too  loud so i adjusted to max out at 60  percent on the fan speeds and i still  got basically the same performance for  the cpu and even when exporting 8k video  so that impressed me so if you're gonna  buy one of these go and adjust your fan  curves for yourself to lower the fan  noise dramatically now as far as the  gaming performance we have that rtx 3080  just like in the razer but this one you  can actually unlock it to 165 watts i  believe out of the box at 140 compared  to the razer which is like 105 or so and  with this cooling system it could  actually power it and thanks to that if  you are gaming which is a great gaming  laptop you should get noticeably better  frame rate compared to that razer now if  you unplug this laptop those frame rates  drop dramatically and you're limited to  basically 30 fps now you can go in and  disable nvidia battery boost that will  give you up to 60 but that is all that  you can do in order to save battery life  with this system so that just shows us  that this is more of a mobile  workstation kind of mobile desktop  that's lightweight and all-in-one  because in those times when you want  full performance you have to plug it in  now on the flip side actually gets about  six and a half to seven hours of regular  web use compared to about four with that  razor so that definitely does help and  of course the razer also throttles down  if you unplug it now for productivity  when we exported 50 images it was  slightly slower than the razer laptop  with the i9 so as far as cpu performance  it does seem to be slightly slower than  that of course you have the cost benefit  but when you go to something like super  resolution which uses the graphics we  saw a significant improvement thanks to  the unlocked 165 watts for the 3080. i  saw a similar thing for video editing  when we exported 8k video it was  slightly slower than the razer and that  is due to the cpu kind of limiting the  3080 not a significant chunk but it's  noticeable but then when we go to other  tasks that are more graphics based for  example 6k black magic raw it is faster  than the razer and the same thing goes  for other tasks like denoising video in  davinci resolve we do see a noticeable  frame rate increase in that so you are  getting better graphics performance so  overall there is a lot of power packed  into this machine the cpu on battery is  faster than the intel variant when it's  plugged in it is slightly slower and the  graphics is also  faster as well noticeably faster with  that you get better battery life and of  course you get double the ssds double  the ram  in this machine for a much lower price  point and the one thing that i thought  would be the biggest limitation the  display i actually prefer over the razer  of course webcam isn't great the  speakers aren't great so it really  depends on what you're looking for if  you're in this price range i don't know  of any other laptop that gives you  better performance for the money so if  you care about performance you want a  game with the 165 hertz screen you want  to do some video editing photo editing  it packs a lot of bing for the buck  especially if you need windows and you  use programs that are only windows based  so there you guys go let me know your  thoughts on the vector pro down in the  comments section below check out one of  those great videos right over there  click above to subscribe if you guys  want to help us reach our goal of 1  million subscribers we would greatly  appreciate it this has been max and i'll  catch you in the next video  [Music]

